  "body": {
    "id": "prop:pair-radius",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Suppose \\(n\\ge2\\). Under the two-scale geometric restriction \\(q<\\gamma\\), \\(a\\asymp\\ell^\\alpha\\), \\(b\\asymp\\ell^\\beta\\), \\(ab\\asymp\\delta\\), \\(h\\asymp\\delta^{1/\\gamma}\\), and \\(\\ell\\asymp\\delta^{1/q}\\), sparse occupancy gives \\(kp_2\\asymp n^2h^d\\ell^d\\). Thus the two exponents in Theorem 2 are bounded below by fixed positive constants when \\(\\delta\\ge C\\max\\{r_{\\mathrm{pair}},r_F\\}\\), and can be made arbitrarily large by increasing \\(C\\). This is a fixed-error pure-private separation guarantee, restricted to \\(q<\\gamma\\) and \\(nv\\le c_0\\), at the named coupled radius \\(r_F=(n^2\\epsilon)^{-1/D_F}\\), without a nuisance-sign logarithm. For \\(k=1\\) and nuisance profile \\(\\lambda=(\\lambda_1)\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\), let \\(O_i=(Y_i,A_i,X_i)\\), \\(i\\in\\{1,2\\}\\), be independent records from \\(P_{s,(\\lambda_1)}\\), and set \\(U_i=2A_i-1\\), \\(V_i=2Y_i-1\\), and \\(Z_{12}=(U_1V_2+V_1U_2)/2\\). Then the sanity reduction is \\[\\mathbb E_{P_{s,(\\lambda_1)}^{\\otimes2}}\\!\\left[Z_{12}\\,\\middle|\\,X_1,X_2\\in C_1\\right]=4sab.\\]",
